Aerie-Purpose: Give a page that holds no camera or microphone permission the same short device list from navigator.mediaDevices.enumerateDevices() while fingerprinting protection is on for its site, one audioinput, one audiooutput and one videoinput with empty deviceId, label and groupId, whatever the machine has (issue #202).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: content/browser/renderer_host/media/media_devices_manager.cc (OnDevicesEnumeratedAndRanked), content/browser/media/media_devices_util.cc and media_devices_util.h (MediaDeviceSaltAndOrigin, GetMediaDeviceSaltAndOrigin), content/browser/BUILD.gn
Aerie-Kind: hook

Upstream trims the list of a kind to its first device when the page has no permission for that
kind, and gives that entry an empty id, label and group id (W3C Media Capture access control
model). A machine that has no camera, or no microphone or speaker the system lists, therefore
shows no entry of that kind, and the number of kinds a page sees tells it what the machine has.

The browser decides, per request, in MediaDevicesManager::OnDevicesEnumeratedAndRanked, which has
the permission result of the page for each kind:
- The page holds a grant for the kind (microphone for audioinput and, as upstream decides,
  audiooutput; camera for videoinput): nothing changes. The real devices, ids and labels come back
  as normal.
- The page holds no grant and protection is on for the site
  (MediaDeviceSaltAndOrigin::fingerprinting_protected, false when the HeliumNoise feature is
  off): the list of the kind has exactly one entry,
  with an empty deviceId, label and groupId. When the stock list has one entry already, it is
  untouched. When the machine has no such device, one empty entry is added. The pure code that
  decides, and that says how many entries are missing, is Aerie's own, with unit tests
  (components/aerie_media_devices).

"Protection is on for the site" is ContentBrowserClient::HeliumNoiseEnabled for the top page's
origin, read on the UI thread when GetMediaDeviceSaltAndOrigin makes the salt: the same rule that
decides whether a page gets its noise tokens (the pref, the per-site HELIUM_NOISE setting, and the
HeliumNoise feature). In a private window, and for a top page with an opaque origin (a sandboxed or data: page, which has no site to switch off), the feature and the global pref decide, so the list is pinned. Turning protection off, globally or for the site, or starting the browser
with --disable-features=HeliumNoise, gives back the real list. A frame of another site in the
page gets the answer of its top page, as for the tokens.

The surface has no token and no derived value: the pinned list is the same for every site, so
nothing is derived, stored or sent to a renderer (S1, S2, S5 hold trivially). getUserMedia,
device labels after a grant, the devicechange event and WebRTC address handling are not changed.

